Spec: KubectlCompleteVynilShim

Platform: POSIX shell

Purpose:
  Register the plugin with kubectl's dynamic-completion convention: kubectl discovers
    completions by executing `kubectl_complete-vynil` with the user's partial words and
    parses Cobra `__complete` output, so this file exists solely to forward those words to
    the real binary's completion entry point.

Owns:
  ./kubectl_complete-vynil

Must:
  Start with the `#!/usr/bin/env sh` interpreter line so kubectl executes it through a
    shell without depending on bash.
  Invoke @kubectl-vynil with the first argument `__complete` followed by every forwarded
    argument verbatim and unquoted-expansion-free, through @exec so the shim process is
    replaced and kubectl sees the binary's exit code, stdout (`value[\tdesc]` lines plus
    the directive) and stderr directly.
  Resolve the binary from @PATH (@kubectl-vynil is looked up as bare `kubectl-vynil`); no
    path probing, no fallback candidate, no wrapper logic before the @exec line.
  Emit none of its own bytes on stdout or stderr; every byte kubectl parses originates from
    @kubectl_vynil::completion::@run behind the `__complete` intercept frozen in
    ./src/cli.sdd and ./src/main.sdd.

Depends on:
  ./src/main.sdd — the exact-argv[1] `__complete` intercept that consumes the forwarded tail.
  ./src/completion.sdd — the candidate/directive output format kubectl parses.

Handles:
  Zero forwarded arguments — @kubectl-vynil __complete with no tail still answers the
    top-level candidates and directive per ./src/completion.sdd.
  `kubectl-vynil` absent from @PATH — the shell fails at @exec with its own
    "not found" diagnostic and the kubectl completion simply shows nothing useful; the
    shim does not detect or remap that failure.

Tasks:
  [ ] Decide later whether the shim needs the executable bit preserved by packaging
      (kubectl only executes it by name), and whether a `vynil`-named twin shim is wanted
      for a future rename.

Done when:
  The file is byte-stable at the documented @exec forward and package checks keep it
    mode-executable.

Scenario: kubectl asks for top-level candidates
  Given `kubectlCompleteVynilShim` executes with no arguments
  When the shell reaches @exec
  Then `kubectl-vynil` runs with argv `__complete`
  And kubectl receives exactly the binary's candidate lines and `:4` directive
